To take a snapshot of a VM in KVM when the VM disk is stored on Ceph RBD storage, there are two common methods:

Ceph RBD snapshot (storage-level snapshot)
libvirt/QEMU snapshot (VM-level snapshot)

For production workloads, Ceph RBD snapshots are usually preferred because they are fast and space efficient.

1. Check VM Disk Location

First verify that the VM is using an RBD image.

virsh domblklist <vm-name>

Example:

[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# virsh domblklist vm101
 Target   Source
---------------------
 vda      /dev/rbd0

[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# rbd showmapped
id  pool     namespace  image       snap  device   
0   vm-pool             vm101-disk  -     /dev/rbd0

You can also inspect XML:

# virsh dumpxml vm01

2. Create Ceph RBD Snapshot (Recommended)
Syntax
rbd snap create <pool>/<image>@<snapshot-name>

Example:
[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# rbd snap create vm-pool/vm101-disk@before-upgrade-24052026
Creating snap: 100% complete...done.

[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# rbd snap ls vm-pool/vm101-disk
SNAPID  NAME                     SIZE   PROTECTED  TIMESTAMP               
     3  before-upgrade-24052026  5 GiB             Sun May 24 07:05:34 2026
[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# 

Option A — Freeze filesystem inside VM

Inside guest VM:

fsfreeze -f /

Take snapshot:

rbd snap create rbd-pool/vm-100-disk-0@before-upgrade

Then unfreeze:

***** How to restore VM from Snapshot *****
[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# rbd snap ls vm-pool/vm101-disk
SNAPID  NAME                     SIZE   PROTECTED  TIMESTAMP               
     3  before-upgrade-24052026  5 GiB             Sun May 24 07:05:34 2026

[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# rbd snap rollback vm-pool/vm101-disk@before-upgrade-24052026
Rolling back to snapshot: 0% complete...failed.
rbd: rollback failed: (30) Read-only file system

[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# virsh list --all
 Id   Name    State
------------------------
 -    vm101   shut off

[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# rbd showmapped
id  pool     namespace  image       snap  device   
0   vm-pool             vm101-disk  -     /dev/rbd0

[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# rbd unmap /dev/rbd0
[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# rbd showmapped

[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# rbd snap rollback vm-pool/vm101-disk@before-upgrade-24052026
Rolling back to snapshot: 100% complete...done.

root@kvm-node1 ceph]# rbd -p vm-pool ls --id libvirt
vm101-disk

[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# rbd map vm-pool/vm101-disk --id libvirt
/dev/rbd0
[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# rbd showmapped
id  pool     namespace  image       snap  device   
0   vm-pool             vm101-disk  -     /dev/rbd0
[root@kvm-node1 ceph]# 

fsfreeze -u /
Option B — Use QEMU Guest Agent (Best Practice)

Install guest agent inside VM:

Ubuntu/Debian
apt install qemu-guest-agent
systemctl enable --now qemu-guest-agent
RHEL/CentOS
yum install qemu-guest-agent
systemctl enable --now qemu-guest-agent

Enable in VM XML:

<channel type='unix'>

Then libvirt can create quiesced snapshots.

4. Create Snapshot Using virsh
External snapshot (recommended)
virsh snapshot-create-as \
--domain vm01 \
--name before-upgrade \
--disk-only \
--atomic

If guest agent is installed:

virsh snapshot-create-as \
--domain vm01 \
--name before-upgrade \
--disk-only \
--quiesce \
--atomic
5. Rollback to Snapshot
Stop VM
virsh shutdown vm01
Rollback
rbd snap rollback rbd-pool/vm-100-disk-0@before-upgrade
Start VM
virsh start vm01
6. Delete Snapshot
rbd snap rm rbd-pool/vm-100-disk-0@before-upgrade
7. Important Production Notes
RBD snapshots are copy-on-write, so creation is very fast.
Too many snapshots can reduce performance.
Always test rollback procedure.
For databases like MySQL/PostgreSQL/MongoDB, use application-aware backup if possible.
For long-term backup, export snapshots:
rbd export rbd-pool/vm-100-disk-0@before-upgrade backup.img
8. Check Existing Snapshots for All Images
rbd ls rbd-pool

Loop through:

for img in $(rbd ls rbd-pool); do
    echo "Image: $img"
    rbd snap ls rbd-pool/$img
done
9. If Using qcow2 on CephFS Instead of RBD

Then snapshot method changes:

virsh snapshot-create-as vm01 snap1

or use:

qemu-img snapshot

But for performance and production environments, direct RBD disks are generally better than qcow2 over CephFS.
